  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 315 AM CDT Sun Oct 25 2026\n\nAs of 230 AM Sunday morning, a slow-moving midlevel trough remains \nacross the southwest US with ridging across the central and eastern \nCONUS. A northern stream midlevel shortwave trough was advancing \nacross Ontario with an attendant surface trough bisecting KS from \nsouthwest to northeast. Widely scattered showers continue to reside \nacross far western KS and CO within a broad zone of midlevel WAA and \ngentle height falls. The midlevel height falls will gradually spread \neastward this afternoon and tonight. This will spread rain chances \ninto portions of central KS late this afternoon into the evening. \nThese widely scattered showers will continue tonight and into Monday \nwith the best chances remaining across central and western KS. More-\nwidespread rain shower activity is anticipated Monday afternoon as \nthe main shortwave trough ejects into the central high Plains. Rain \nchances will extend to the I-135 corridor Monday afternoon and even \ntowards southeast KS late Monday night into Tuesday morning a \nsecondary weak shortwave trough lifts from west TX into the Ozarks. \nAs far as rain amounts are concerns, areas generally along and \nsoutheast of the KS Turnpike will see the lightest amounts with \ntotals near or less than 0.10\". As we progress westward and into \ncentral KS, amounts up to 0.50\" are possible, especially across \nBarton and Russell counties. \n\nTransitioning into midweek, broad southwesterly midlevel flow will \nremain as another western CONUS trough deepens. Broad low and \nmidlevel WAA may continue to yield isolated to scattered showers \nespecially at night. By weeks-end and into next week, a series of \nweak perturbations may eject across the area, resulting in \nintermittent rain chances.",
